@Subroutines
.
=SUBROUTINES
.
.A SUB runs its body and has no value. A FUNCTION has a value and is used in
.an expression.
.
.SUB name(parameters) ... ENDSUB. ENDSUB can be written END SUB. A SUB is
.called by its name and its brackets. CALL in front is optional. The brackets
.are not: they make the name a call, even with no arguments.
.
:SUB SHOW(N AS UBYTE)
:  PRINT N
:ENDSUB
:SHOW(7)
:CALL SHOW(7)
.
.  - A parameter is a BYTE, UBYTE, WORD, UWORD or STRING. A number parameter
.    needs AS: SUB SHOW(N) stops the compile with MISSING AS: N. A STRING
.    parameter takes the $ mark or AS STRING.
.  - An argument is worked into its parameter's type: a UWORD into a UBYTE
.    parameter keeps its low byte. A STRING parameter has no size, and the
.    SUB works on the caller's string. See Strings.
.  - The number of arguments must match the parameters.
.  - RETURN leaves the SUB at once. In a SUB, RETURN has nothing after it.
.  - A SUB name with a $, or an AS type after the brackets, stops the compile
.    with SUB WITH A TYPE: USE FUNCTION.
.  - A SUB or a FUNCTION may be called before it is written.
.  - The main program jumps over a SUB or a FUNCTION, so either can sit
.    anywhere outside a block.
.  - A SUB or a FUNCTION cannot call itself. One that calls itself stops the
.    compile with A SUB CANNOT CALL ITSELF.
.  - SUBs and FUNCTIONs cannot call each other. A loop of calls between them,
.    A calls B and B calls A or a longer loop, stops the compile with SUBS
.    CALL EACH OTHER at the line of one of the calls.
.
=FUNCTION
.
.FUNCTION name(parameters) AS type ... ENDFUNCTION. ENDFUNCTION can be
.written END FUNCTION. The parameters and the call are as in a SUB. AS after
.the brackets gives the type of the value. RETURN gives the value.
.
:FUNCTION MAX(A AS UWORD, B AS UWORD) AS UWORD
:  IF A > B THEN RETURN A
:  RETURN B
:ENDFUNCTION
:FUNCT HALF(N AS UWORD) AS UWORD
:  RETURN N / 2
:END FUNCT
:DIM T AS UWORD
:T = MAX(3, HALF(100))
.
.  - A number FUNCTION with no AS stops the compile with MISSING AS: name. A
.    STRING FUNCTION may take the $ mark in place of AS, as in STRING results
.    below.
.  - FUNCT is FUNCTION and ENDFUNCT is ENDFUNCTION, so END FUNCT is END
.    FUNCTION. Every source has them, as if it began with BIND FUNCT FUNCTION
.    and BIND ENDFUNCT ENDFUNCTION.
.  - A FUNCTION needs at least one RETURN with a value. Its end, when the
.    body reaches it, gives 0, or "" in a STRING FUNCTION.
.  - A FUNCTION may be called as a statement. The value is lost.
.  - The end must match the start. END SUB or ENDSUB in a FUNCTION stops the
.    compile with ENDSUB IN A FUNCTION. END FUNCTION or ENDFUNCTION in a SUB
.    stops it with ENDFUNCTION IN A SUB.
.  - END FUNCTION outside a SUB or a FUNCTION stops the compile with
.    ENDFUNCTION WITHOUT FUNCTION.
.  - A FUNCTION still open at the end of the source stops the compile with
.    FUNCTION WITHOUT ENDFUNCTION at the FUNCTION line. A SUB gives SUB
.    WITHOUT ENDSUB.
.
=STRING results
.
.A FUNCTION that returns a STRING has the $ mark or AS STRING: FUNCTION
.FIRST3$(S$) or FUNCTION WRAP(T AS STRING) AS STRING. Its value is assigned,
.printed, compared and joined with + as any string is.
.
:DIM A$ * 20
:FUNCTION FIRST3$(S$)
:  RETURN MID$(S$, 1, 3)
:ENDFUNCTION
:A$ = FIRST3$("HELLO")
:PRINT FIRST3$("WORLD") + "!"
.
.  - A STRING result is at most 255 characters. An assignment cuts it to the
.    size of the destination.
.  - A STRING result passed to a SUB or a FUNCTION is passed as a copy.
.
=Locals
.
.Parameters, and names DIMed in a SUB or a FUNCTION, are its locals. In this
.section SUB means either. A local with the same name as a main program
.variable hides it inside the SUB. Main program variables DIMed before the
.SUB can be used in it. Labels belong to the SUB too, so no GOTO crosses into
.or out of one.
.
.  - A local does not keep its value between calls.
.  - A UBYTE or UWORD local, and a local array, starts undefined. Set it
.    before you read it.
.  - A local string is set empty each time its DIM line runs.
.  - Only the SUB itself can name its locals. Inside SHOW, SHOW.N is N.
.    Outside SHOW, PRINT SHOW.N stops the compile with LOCAL TO A SUB:
.    SHOW.N.
.  - The locals of one SUB take at most 16384 bytes. The DIM that passes it
.    stops the compile with LOCALS TOO BIG.
.  - The SUBs of one level of the call graph share the memory of their
.    locals. The compiler does not see a call through a pointer or SYS. A SUB
.    called that way may share that memory with a SUB that is running.
.  - The main program's variables are not locals. Each has its own address
.    and keeps its value.
.
